$$\\begin{cases} 3x + 2y = 29 \\\\ x - 2y = -1 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$4$"},{"key":"B","text":"$7$"},{"key":"C","text":"$10$"},{"key":"D","text":"$15$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(3x + 2y) + (x - 2y) = 29 + (-1)$, giving $4x = 28$, so $x = 7$. Substituting back: $7 - 2y = -1$ gives $y = 4$. The value $4$ is the value of $y$, not $x$, reflecting a misread of which variable the question asks for. The value $10$ arises from an equation setup error such as adding $29 + 1 = 30$ and dividing by $3$ instead of $4$. The value $15$ results from an arithmetic slip such as computing $29 - (-1) = 30$ and then $30 + 28 = 58$, divided by some incorrect factor.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);












$$\\begin{cases} 5x + 9y = 17 \\\\ 15x + cy = 34 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$18$"},{"key":"B","text":"$27$"},{"key":"C","text":"$34$"},{"key":"D","text":"$45$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'For a system to have no solution, the equations must represent parallel lines: the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ must be proportional but the constants must not be. The first equation has coefficients $5$ and $9$. The second equation has $15 = 3 \\times 5$ for $x$, so the multiplier is $3$. For parallel lines, $c = 3 \\times 9 = 27$. With $c = 27$, the second equation would need a constant of $3 \\times 17 = 51$ for infinitely many solutions, but the constant is $34 \\neq 51$, confirming no solution. The value $18$ comes from multiplying $9$ by $2$ instead of matching the ratio $15/5 = 3$. The value $34$ confuses the constant term on the right side with the coefficient being solved for. The value $45$ results from multiplying $15 \\times 3 = 45$, applying the multiplier to the $x$-coefficient of the second equation instead of to the $y$-coefficient of the first.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);











Text 2: Historian Lena Morales acknowledges the role of financial incentives in railroad expansion but emphasizes that military strategy was an equally powerful driver. She notes that federal officials viewed railroads as essential for transporting troops and supplies to remote frontier outposts and that several key routes were selected specifically to serve military objectives rather than to maximize commercial traffic.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that economic motives were important but arguing that they were not the sole driving force behind railroad expansion."},{"key":"B","text":"By rejecting the idea that railroad companies received government subsidies during the nineteenth century."},{"key":"C","text":"By proposing that railroad expansion was primarily driven by the desire to promote tourism in the American West."},{"key":"D","text":"By claiming that economic motives became significant only after the initial phase of railroad construction was complete."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2''s author acknowledges the role of financial incentives but argues that military strategy was an equally powerful driver, indicating that she would accept the economic motive claim while insisting it is incomplete. The choice about rejecting the idea of government subsidies is incorrect because Text 2 explicitly acknowledges the role of financial incentives. The choice about tourism is incorrect because neither text discusses tourism, and nothing in Text 2 supports this claim. The choice about economic motives becoming significant only later is incorrect because Text 2 does not argue that economic motives were absent early on; it argues that military considerations were equally important throughout.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$l