Text 2: Anthropologist Marcus Healy agrees that transcription inevitably alters oral narratives but disputes the implication that written versions are therefore valueless. He points to the endangered status of many oral traditions worldwide and argues that without written documentation, entire narrative ecosystems will vanish as elder storytellers die. Healy concedes that transcriptions are imperfect artifacts but maintains that an imperfect record is preferable to no record at all, particularly when the alternative is irreversible cultural loss.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 accepts a premise of Text 1's argument but challenges the conclusion that follows from it, prioritizing preservation over fidelity."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides empirical evidence that directly disproves the central claim made in Text 1 about the nature of oral storytelling."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 extends Text 1's argument by proposing a method of transcription that would preserve the improvisational quality of oral performance."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 rejects Text 1's characterization of Western folklorists as biased and defends their methodological approach to oral traditions."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2''s author agrees with Text 1''s premise that transcription inevitably alters oral narratives but argues that preservation outweighs the loss of fidelity, especially given the endangered status of many traditions. This means Text 2 accepts the premise but challenges the conclusion. The choice about empirical evidence disproving Text 1 is incorrect because Text 2 does not dispute that transcription distorts oral traditions; it concedes that transcriptions are imperfect. The choice about proposing a new transcription method is incorrect because Text 2 does not suggest any specific methodology for better capturing improvisation. The choice about defending Western folklorists is incorrect because Text 2 does not address Chandrasekaran''s critique of Western folklorists or defend their methods; it focuses on the pragmatic case for documentation.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





- Noam Chomsky''s theory of universal grammar posits that all human languages share certain structural properties, including recursive embedding of clauses.
- Daniel Everett, a linguist who spent over 30 years studying the Pirahã people of the Brazilian Amazon, claims that the Pirahã language lacks recursive syntactic structures.
- The Pirahã language has only 11 phonemes (3 vowels and 8 consonants).
- Everett''s claims have been disputed by other linguists, including Andrew Nevins, David Pesetsky, and Cilene Rodrigues, who argue that Everett mischaracterizes Pirahã syntax.
- The Pirahã people number fewer than 800 individuals.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Although Chomsky's universal grammar theory holds that recursive clause embedding is a property shared by all human languages, linguist Daniel Everett has argued, based on over 30 years of fieldwork with the Pirahã of the Brazilian Amazon, that Pirahã lacks recursive syntactic structures entirely."},{"key":"B","text":"Daniel Everett spent over 30 years studying the Pirahã people of the Brazilian Amazon and found that their language has only 11 phonemes, which is fewer than most other languages, and he claims it also lacks recursion."},{"key":"C","text":"The Pirahã language, spoken by fewer than 800 people in the Brazilian Amazon, has been at the center of a debate involving Noam Chomsky, Daniel Everett, and linguists Andrew Nevins, David Pesetsky, and Cilene Rodrigues."},{"key":"D","text":"Noam Chomsky's theory of universal grammar, which posits that all languages share recursive embedding, has been disputed by Andrew Nevins, David Pesetsky, and Cilene Rodrigues, who criticize Daniel Everett's analysis of the Pirahã language."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal requires two rhetorical moves: acknowledging the widely held view (Chomsky''s universal grammar and recursion) and introducing Everett''s counter-evidence. The option beginning "Although Chomsky''s universal grammar theory" uses a concessive clause to acknowledge the mainstream view and then introduces Everett''s fieldwork-based counter-claim, accomplishing both moves precisely. The option about Everett''s 30 years of study focuses on phoneme count and mentions the recursion claim only briefly, without first acknowledging Chomsky''s theory as the widely held view. The option about the Pirahã language being spoken by fewer than 800 people summarizes the debate''s participants but neither acknowledges the mainstream view nor introduces the specific counter-evidence. The option about Nevins, Pesetsky, and Rodrigues misattributes the dispute: it presents those linguists as disputing Chomsky rather than Everett, reversing the relationship described in the notes.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-27"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-27","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

